two-resistors-pair-answer-why = In a series circuit the current has one road: as many charges pass through the first resistor as through the second — otherwise they would pile up somewhere. So the current is the same everywhere. The voltage is the work the source does carrying charge round the circuit; the charge “spends” that work on the way: part across the first resistor, the rest across the second. So U = U₁ + U₂. By Ohm’s law U₁ = I·R₁, U₂ = I·R₂: twice the resistance, twice the voltage. That is how a string of tree lights wired one after another shares the mains voltage — and if one burns out, all go dark.
two-resistors-pair-answer-why-2 = In a parallel circuit both resistors are joined to the same two points, so the voltage across them is the same — the voltage between those points. The current, reaching the fork, splits: charges neither vanish nor appear, so as much flows into the junction as out: I = I₁ + I₂. The branch of smaller resistance carries more current (I = U/R): half the resistance, twice the current. Each new branch adds another road for the current, so the total resistance is less than the smallest branch’s. That is how appliances at home are wired: each gets the mains voltage and works independently of the others.
two-resistors-pair-answer-text = The experiment is not finished yet.
two-resistors-pair-answer-text-2 = The current is the same everywhere — { $I }. The voltage across the 2 Ω resistor is { $U }, across the 4 Ω one { $U2 }, across both { $U3 }. The sum U₁ + U₂ = { $U4 } — the same total voltage (any difference is within the voltmeter’s 0.2 V division). The larger resistance gets the larger voltage: as many times more resistance, as many times more voltage.
two-resistors-pair-answer-text-3 = The voltage across both branches is the same — { $U } (with the ammeter before the fork the voltmeter reads a little less — { $U2 }: the ammeter itself “takes” part of the voltage). The current in the branch of the 2 Ω resistor is { $I }, of the 4 Ω one { $I2 }, before the fork { $I3 }. The sum I₁ + I₂ = { $I4 } — almost the same total: the small difference comes from the ammeter’s 0.05 A division and from its own small resistance, which, put in a branch, lowers the current in it a little. The branch of half the resistance carries twice the current.
